**New evidence**
- `THAILAND’S BUDGET IN BRIEF FISCAL YEAR 2022` / Budget Bureau budget brief, official program framing for FY2022.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"THAILAND’S BUDGET IN BRIEF FISCAL YEAR 2022","publisher":"Budget Bureau, Thailand","publication_date":"2022-01-28","url":"https://pubhtml5.com/tvob/cfiz/basic/","claim":"The FY2022 budget brief says the annual budget is a plan for disbursing state funds and managing monetary and fiscal policy, and that budget policy prioritizes integrated allocation, cutting low-priority projects, reducing social inequality, stimulating economic expansion, public participation, and vulnerable groups.","locator":"pp. 230-259; especially lines 230-257 and 270-280 in the text version","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"It is a mirrored copy of the official Budget Bureau brief and the text is internally consistent and detailed, but the host is not the originating publisher.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct FY2022 program document within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level budget policy under Prayut's government; strong attribution to his administration, weaker for his personal authorship.","contrary_evidence":["The document is a plan, not proof of implementation or outcome.","External shocks still shaped FY2022 execution."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.10"]}
- `THAILAND’S BUDGET IN BRIEF FISCAL YEAR 2022` / same budget brief, specific social-allocation strategy.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"THAILAND’S BUDGET IN BRIEF FISCAL YEAR 2022","publisher":"Budget Bureau, Thailand","publication_date":"2022-01-28","url":"https://pubhtml5.com/tvob/cfiz/basic/","claim":"The FY2022 budget brief allocates 731,707.4 million baht, or 23.6 percent of the budget, to create opportunities and reduce inequality, including public welfare cards, debt relief, social security, housing loans, public health, education, and support for vulnerable groups.","locator":"lines 378-416 in the text version","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed budget text mirrors the official brief and provides concrete allocations, though hosted on a third-party flipbook site.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"FY2022 allocation document within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet and budget-system attribution is strong; direct personal attribution to Prayut is moderate because this is an agency document rather than his own speech.","contrary_evidence":["Large budget lines do not show execution quality by themselves.","Some spending was still broad-based and not clearly targeted enough to avoid leakages."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.10"]}
- `Thailand approves tax cut on diesel to ease high prices` / Reuters via Investing.com.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand approves tax cut on diesel to ease high prices","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/thailand-approves-tax-cut-on-diesel-to-ease-high-prices-2764110","claim":"The cabinet cut the diesel excise tax by 3 baht per litre for three months to ease high energy prices and support the price cap on diesel.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'BANGKOK (Reuters) - Thailand's cabinet...'","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is strong for contemporaneous reporting of cabinet decisions and the claim is specific and time-stamped.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and tied to the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet action under Prayut; direct authority is strong though implementation depended on finance and energy ministries.","contrary_evidence":["The measure was temporary and did not resolve underlying inflation and energy dependence.","It was a defensive response to external price shocks rather than a structural fix."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.9"]}
- `Thailand approves tax breaks for EVs, 'high potential' foreigners` / Reuters via Investing.com.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand approves tax breaks for EVs, 'high potential' foreigners","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thailand-approves-tax-breaks-for-evs-high-potential-foreigners-2769072","claim":"The cabinet approved EV tax incentives and lower taxes for skilled foreign professionals, saying the EV package was expected to add 7,000 EVs in the first year and to stimulate investment and spending.","locator":"paragraphs beginning 'Thailand's cabinet on Tuesday approved...' and 'The government will cut excise tax...'","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reports a precise cabinet policy with explicit numerical expectations and contemporaneous attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and directly about the Prayut cabinet's economic program.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level policy under Prayut; shared with finance ministry and economic agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The gains were forecasted, not independently verified in the article.","The plan also relied on attracting foreigners, which may not reflect broad domestic productivity gains."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
- `IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand` / IMF.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/13/pr22195-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-thailand","claim":"IMF staff said Thailand's recovery was being supported by a comprehensive policy package and accelerated vaccination, that policy support should become increasingly targeted, and that broad energy subsidies should be phased out in favor of targeted support for vulnerable groups and productivity-enhancing investment.","locator":"paragraphs 247-264, especially 247-260","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF staff statement with clear policy recommendations and contemporaneous assessment of the Thai authorities' response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and about the target ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Thai authorities' policy response under Prayut's government; not solely to Prayut personally.","contrary_evidence":["IMF said the recovery was uneven and inflation was rising.","The statement is advisory and partially forward-looking, not a pure outcome audit."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- `IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Thailand` / IMF.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/09/01/pr22299-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-thailand","claim":"IMF Executive Directors said the authorities' bold and appropriate policy response helped maintain macroeconomic and financial stability and facilitate a growth rebound, while recommending that untargeted energy subsidies be gradually replaced with targeted support.","locator":"Executive Board Assessment section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF Board statement summarizing post-mission assessment and policy direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and directly evaluating the period's policy response.","ruler_attribution":"State-policy attribution is strong; the result reflects cabinet, central bank, and fiscal authorities working under Prayut's administration.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also said recovery was fragile and uneven.","The statement praises the policy mix but does not prove all intended reforms were implemented."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- `Thailand’s Economy Remains Resilient amid Global Headwinds` / World Bank.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand’s Economy Remains Resilient amid Global Headwinds","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/14/thailand-s-economy-remains-resilient-amid-global-headwinds","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's economy was projected to recover to pre-pandemic levels in 2022, growth accelerated to 4.5 percent in Q3 on private consumption, tourism, and authorities' cost-of-living measures, and the fiscal response to COVID-19 significantly mitigated household welfare losses.","locator":"lines 144-152 in the article text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank press release with specific macro and distributional claims and clear publication date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year and summarizing 2022 performance.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Prayut's administration, but also to reopening, tourism recovery, and global conditions; attribution is mixed.","contrary_evidence":["The same source says poverty was projected to rise to 6.6 percent in 2022.","External headwinds and phased-out relief limited the strength of the rebound."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- `Rural Thailand Faces the Largest Poverty Challenges with High Income Inequality` / World Bank.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rural Thailand Faces the Largest Poverty Challenges with High Income Inequality","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/21/rural-thailand-faces-the-largest-poverty-challenges-with-high-income-inequality","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's poverty reduction had slowed since 2015, 79 percent of the poor remained in rural areas, rural households were heavily hit by income losses after March 2020, and inequality remained the highest in East Asia and the Pacific.","locator":"press release body, especially the opening and middle paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank press release with concrete rural-poverty and inequality findings based on a diagnostic study.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and relevant to the end-of-year trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: reflects inherited structure, COVID shock, and policy limits under Prayut's government rather than a single decision.","contrary_evidence":["This is more a constraint/outcome diagnosis than a clean ruler-performance finding.","It does not isolate what portion of the problem Prayut could realistically have solved in 2022."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
- `Thai PM Survives 4th No-Confidence Vote in Parliament` / Reuters via VOA.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai PM Survives 4th No-Confidence Vote in Parliament","publisher":"VOA News / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-07-23","url":"https://www.voanews.com/a/thai-pm-survives-4th-no-confidence-vote-in-parliament-/6670722.html","claim":"Prayuth survived a no-confidence vote with 256 votes in favor and 206 against, after four days of public grilling over corruption and economic mismanagement.","locator":"paragraphs 94-99","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting republished by VOA with precise vote totals and context on the coalition's parliamentary strength.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and squarely within the ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Prayut's coalition management and parliamentary control, though not proof of policy success.","contrary_evidence":["The opposition framed the vote as about corruption and economic mismanagement.","Survival in parliament does not by itself show effective governance."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- `Opposition ups pressure on Prayut as coalition misses House quorum again` / The Nation.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Opposition ups pressure on Prayut as coalition misses House quorum again","publisher":"The Nation","publication_date":"2022-02-11","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/in-focus/40012226","claim":"The PPRP-led coalition failed to make quorum, adjourning the House for the 17th time, and the article describes the coalition as fragile and internally disunited.","locator":"lines 19-25 and 36-43","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting with concrete parliamentary detail; less authoritative than Reuters but still specific and time-locked.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within the target year and directly about coalition functionality.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Prayut's governing coalition and his ability to keep legislators aligned, though factional autonomy matters.","contrary_evidence":["Attendance failure may reflect MPs' behavior rather than direct personal action by Prayut.","The article also notes the coalition and opposition both share responsibility for House dysfunction."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}
- `Prayuth, all other censured ministers survive no-confidence vote` / Thai Newsroom.
  SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prayuth, all other censured ministers survive no-confidence vote","publisher":"Thai Newsroom","publication_date":"2022-07-23","url":"https://thainewsroom.com/2022/07/23/prayut-all-other-censured-ministers-survive-no-confidence-vote/","claim":"Prayuth and 10 ministers survived the censure motion, with 256 votes of confidence against 206 no-confidence votes, and the article says the coalition remained intact after the vote.","locator":"opening paragraphs and vote tally section","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"It reports a specific parliamentary result, but is a secondary local outlet with less authority than Reuters.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and directly relevant to coalition control.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Prayut's coalition discipline and parliamentary bargaining, with major influence from coalition partners.","contrary_evidence":["The article explicitly says the vote was widely expected to be followed by a cabinet reshuffle or more bargaining.","Survival does not show strong execution in policy delivery."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 30-plus documents across official Thai budget pages, IMF/World Bank reports, Reuters/VOA/Nation coverage, and a smaller set of secondary Thai-language items. The useful set centered on:
- Official budget framing: Budget Bureau FY2022 brief and FY2023 budget page.
- Macro-policy and outcome reports: IMF June and September 2022, World Bank October and December 2022.
- Concrete policy actions: diesel tax cuts, EV incentives, debt management plan, targeted support measures.
- Political control/coordination: no-confidence vote, coalition quorum failures, coalition fragmentation.
Removed or deprioritized items included:
- IMF eLibrary full report attempts that hit a 403 block, replaced by the IMF press release and country-report summary.
- Reuters Connect image-only items with no substantive text.
- Thai Newsroom rewrites when Reuters originals existed for the same event.
- 2023-plus sources that were outside the target year unless they were clearly retrospective on 2022.
- Broad rights/media-freedom reports that were useful context but did not add much to the 8B causal chain beyond what was already captured in prior chapters.
- Generic budget-bureau navigation pages and homepage links that proved the document existed but did not add substantive 8B evidence.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct execution/audit evidence for specific FY2022 budget lines is still thin. The dossier has strong program and policy evidence, but less hard proof on delivery quality at ministry/program level.
- Attribution remains mixed on several outcome claims because tourism reopening, global commodity prices, the central bank, and preexisting pandemic policy all mattered alongside Prayut's cabinet.
- More search on audit reports, budget execution summaries, or ministry performance reviews would help sharpen 8B.8 and 8B.10.
- If you want the dossier made sharper, the next best additions would be a budget-execution audit, a sector-specific implementation evaluation, or a more direct record of personnel changes after underperformance.
